"""Original introductory co-design; no source model or instrument adapted."""
def build(c,m):
 return c(64,'Inclusive Local Planning and Co Design','Inclusive Local Planning\nand Co Design','Prepare a fictional local planning proposal that shares bounded decisions, compares feasible options, checks inclusion and records revisions without claiming causal outcomes or legal compliance.',[
 m('Shared planning and decision scope','Clarify who can shape a local plan and what remains conditional.',[
'Local planning links a purpose with choices about activities, resources and responsibilities in a particular place. Co-design implies that relevant people can shape meaningful aspects of those choices, rather than simply comment on a finished proposal. Explain which decisions are open, which constraints are fixed and who holds final authority. A funder may restrict the budget while leaving session location or delivery arrangements open. OECD guidance encourages matching participation to the decision and explaining its intended influence. Do not label an information session as shared design merely because residents were invited to hear it. [S32]',
'Inclusion requires attention to whose knowledge enters the plan and whose preferences carry weight. A formal association may provide useful connections without representing people who are not members. Consider language, disability, timing, care responsibilities, employment patterns and digital access as questions to check rather than assumed group characteristics. IACD practice standards connect planning, engagement and diversity with community development. Their role here is a competency comparator, not a prescribed course syllabus or proof that a particular participation method will work locally. Document missing perspectives and the conditions needed to engage them appropriately. [S31]',
'Sharing power is affected by more than the number of invited participants. Access to information, the ability to suggest alternatives, authority to change a draft and control over the final account all matter. The UK synthesis of research co-production identifies institutional constraints and relational negotiation in that specialised setting. It cannot establish the effectiveness of a local planning meeting in Nigeria, but it challenges the assumption that a friendly atmosphere guarantees influence. A fictional design note should explain how contributions can lead to an actual revision and how participants will learn what happened to their suggestions. [R42, S32]',
'Keep the scope introductory. Learners develop an invented service arrangement, not a legal compliance assessment or an advanced research protocol. Do not seek personal histories, recruit real residents or ask children to test an idea. An alternative fictional exercise can meet the same outcome. State which local practice, accessibility and safeguarding questions need qualified review before any real activity. A clear boundary can improve the honesty of the proposal even when it limits what can be promised. A plan is a draft until the responsible authority has approved the necessary decisions and resources. [S31, S32]'],
'A fictional local programme calls its launch event co-design, but the location, time and delivery method were already decided. Only the poster colour can change.',
'Write a revised scope statement naming meaningful open choices, constraints, authority and an accessible route for missing perspectives.',
'If meaningful decisions cannot change, describe the event honestly as information or consultation. Do not promise shared control that participants do not have.', ['S32','S31','R42'],[
('Co-design requires influence over meaningful choices.','A fictional launch lets residents choose only a poster colour. What claim needs caution?','That the programme was meaningfully co-designed.',['That an event was announced.','That a colour can be chosen.','That constraints should be explained.'],'Participation labels should match actual influence.'),
('Fixed constraints should be disclosed.','A grant fixes the budget but delivery times can change. What should participants know?','The budget constraint and the genuine scope to shape delivery.',['That they control every grant condition.','Nothing until after drafting.','That all alternatives are approved.'],'Honest scope enables informed participation.'),
('Formal membership does not exhaust relevant perspectives.','Only association members review a local plan. What remains uncertain?','The views and access needs of affected non-members.',['Whether invited members exist.','Whether all non-members agree.','That representation needs no checking.'],'Affected people may lie outside formal organisations.'),
('Friendly relations do not guarantee shared authority.','A warm meeting leaves all decisions with the original author. What should be examined?','Whether participants could actually revise the plan.',['Whether refreshments prove power sharing.','Whether attendance certifies inclusion.','Whether the author needs no accountability.'],'Influence requires decision arrangements, not atmosphere alone.'),
('Introductory planning does not authorise field recruitment.','A learner proposes recruiting real children to test a draft. What is appropriate here?','Use an invented scenario and identify qualified review dependencies.',['Recruit before approval to save time.','Collect personal histories as design evidence.','Treat a quiz pass as research permission.'],'The course uses fictional exercises with explicit scope limits.')]),
 m('Options resources and inclusive trade-offs','Compare feasible local options while keeping unequal burdens visible.',[
'Planning often involves several plausible options rather than one obvious answer. Describe each option in relation to the same purpose and constraints. Compare access, resource needs, delivery responsibilities and the people who may benefit or bear costs. An option that reaches more people may still exclude a smaller group with a substantial barrier. OECD participation guidance supports clear information and appropriate methods, but it does not remove the need for judgement about trade-offs. Use plain explanations so the comparison can be questioned rather than conceal assumptions in an unexplained score. [S32]',
'Feasibility includes time, paid and unpaid work, transport, materials, competence, approval and continuing support. Do not treat donated labour as unlimited or available without agreement. The Newham ABCD evaluation highlights connecting work and contextual resources, while its cost analysis does not prove cost-effectiveness. A fictional plan should identify the owner and status of each important resource: confirmed for the exercise, assumed or requiring future verification. Avoid solving every gap by asking already constrained people to contribute more time. A missing dependency can justify changing the scope rather than disguising the shortfall. [R41, S31]',
'Choices about local contributions can affect cooperation and expectations. The Tanzanian qualitative comparison raises questions about upfront effort and uncertain funding, but differences between implementing actors and goods limit causal interpretation. Apply this as a prompt to disclose burdens and conditions, not as a rule that all competition undermines development. If residents are asked to help before a grant decision, explain what remains uncertain and what happens if the project cannot proceed. No classroom proposal can guarantee public funding, waive local requirements or bind an external organisation to deliver support. [R43]',
'A reasoned trade-off record explains why an option is proposed, which alternatives were considered and which disadvantages remain. Provide a way for affected people to question the assumptions under approved arrangements. Do not claim that the majority preference resolves every inclusion concern, or that an average benefit compensates automatically for serious exclusion. Keep sensitive individual circumstances out of the learner record; use invented aggregate assumptions and state uncertainty. This task develops transparent planning judgement, not an advanced causal analysis, a legal ruling or authority to make decisions for a real community. [S31, S32]'],
'A fictional team compares a central daytime session with two smaller evening sessions. The first is cheaper but excludes shift workers; the second depends on unconfirmed volunteer support.',
'Compare both options by purpose, access, resources, burdens and unresolved dependencies, then justify a conditional recommendation.',
'Explain the access trade-off and the unconfirmed support. A conditional recommendation is more honest than declaring either option universally inclusive.', ['S32','R41','S31','R43'],[
('Compare options against a shared purpose.','Two fictional delivery options are judged on unrelated criteria. What improves the comparison?','Use the same purpose, access and feasibility questions for both.',['Choose the more colourful poster.','Hide each option\'s costs.','Assume all options have identical users.'],'A common basis makes trade-offs understandable.'),
('Lower cost does not automatically mean inclusive value.','A cheap daytime session excludes shift workers. What should remain visible?','The exclusion alongside cost and feasible alternatives.',['That the lowest cost proves equity.','That excluded people consented.','That access is irrelevant to planning.'],'Resource efficiency and inclusion are distinct considerations.'),
('Unconfirmed support is a dependency.','A fictional option requires volunteers who have not agreed. What status is appropriate?','Conditional on voluntary support or a suitable alternative.',['Fully resourced and approved.','Completed implementation.','Guaranteed because the plan names them.'],'Naming a resource does not secure it.'),
('Contribution burdens should be disclosed.','Residents are asked to work before funding is decided. What belongs in the plan?','The uncertainty, burden and consequence if funding fails.',['A guaranteed benefit without approval.','A hidden condition for unrelated support.','A claim that all competition is harmful.'],'Informed contributions need realistic conditions.'),
('Majority preference does not erase exclusion.','Most invented respondents prefer an inaccessible venue. What is still necessary?','Consider barriers and reasonable feasible alternatives through review.',['Treat all access concerns as settled.','Delete minority reasons.','Claim a legal ruling from the vote.'],'Planning should retain unequal impacts for accountable judgement.')]),
 m('Prototypes feedback and revision','Use a fictional prototype to review usability without confusing a pilot with proof.',[
'A prototype is a provisional representation used to explore an idea, such as an invented session schedule, a plain-language notice or a sample access route. It should be clearly labelled as a draft so nobody mistakes it for an approved service offer. The purpose of reviewing it is to expose unclear assumptions and practical barriers before committing resources. OECD participation guidance connects suitable methods and feedback with decision scope. A simple fictional prototype is sufficient for this course; learners are not asked to launch a service, collect real complaints or recruit vulnerable participants. [S32]',
'Feedback questions should relate to the intended purpose. Ask what the notice makes clear, what is missing, which access condition remains problematic and what would need qualified review. Avoid treating praise as evidence that every relevant person can use the plan. A review by classmates playing invented roles is a simulation, not a representative community consultation. IACD learning principles support revising practice in light of experience, but the claim made about that experience must match the exercise. Record the proposed correction, the reason, the responsible role and whether approval is still required. [S31, S32]',
'Testing and piloting have limits. A small review may identify an ambiguous instruction or a scheduling issue without showing whether the programme improves development outcomes. Outcome claims require suitable measures, context and evidence beyond a successful rehearsal. The qualitative co-production synthesis highlights ongoing negotiation rather than a simple finished state; its findings do not validate this prototype. Be explicit about the difference between a usable draft, an approved plan, delivered activity and demonstrated benefit. Do not relabel a learner\'s simulated exercise as completed field placement or independent professional competence. [R42, S31]',
'A revision history helps people understand what changed and why. Keep the version, decision owner, reason and unresolved issue in an appropriate record, and explain how feedback will reach those who contributed through approved arrangements. If a suggestion cannot be used, give a clear reason instead of silently removing it. Preserve privacy by using invented details in coursework and proportionate aggregate summaries in any future authorised process. Accessibility, local practice, safeguarding, supervised skill observation and learner piloting remain pending before real delivery. A polished document cannot substitute for these decisions or create an academy or provider approval. [S32, S31]'],
'A fictional prototype notice is praised by three classmates. Its author claims statewide consultation, proven outcomes and approval to deliver, although access arrangements are unresolved.',
'Write a revision log with the actual simulation status, one access correction, reason, owner and remaining approval dependency.',
'Call the review a fictional classroom simulation. Distinguish a revised draft from approval, delivery and outcome evidence; retain the unresolved access question.', ['S32','S31','R42'],[
('Prototypes must be labelled as provisional.','An invented notice resembles a real approved offer. What should be explicit?','Its draft simulation status and unresolved approvals.',['A fabricated provider ID.','A guarantee of service access.','A claim that design equals delivery.'],'Clear status prevents mistaken expectations.'),
('Classroom role-play is not community representation.','Three classmates review an invented schedule. What is an accurate description?','A simulated review by classmates.',['A statewide consultation.','A random sample of residents.','Proof that all communities support it.'],'The claim must match who actually participated.'),
('Usability feedback is not outcome proof.','A prototype is easy to understand. What remains unproven?','Whether delivery improves development outcomes.',['That wording can be reviewed.','That users may identify ambiguity.','That a draft can be revised.'],'Usability and programme effects require different evidence.'),
('Revision histories need reasons and owners.','A draft changes after feedback. What should the log retain?','The version, reason, responsible role and unresolved approval status.',['Only the final colour.','Real sensitive histories by default.','An invented signature of approval.'],'Traceability helps reviewers assess decisions.'),
('A simulated task is not completed field placement.','A learner completes a fictional co-design worksheet. What recognition claim is appropriate?','Completion of an introductory learning exercise subject to course review.',['Independent professional competence.','A completed supervised field placement.','Automatic school certification.'],'Learning outputs cannot confer unobserved practice competence.')])])
